---
id: org.dxos.spec.changelog
name: DSL.Changelog
version: 1.0
extends:
  - org.dxos.spec.core
---

The changelog dialect. Adds a `## Changelog` section to a spec: an append-only log of every
decision that changed it, each one attributed to the person or the agent that made it. The body of
a spec says what is true now; the changelog says who decided it, when, and why.

A spec is written by people and agents together. Typically the person gives coarse direction and
the agent fills in the gaps. The two carry different authority, so the log keeps their knowledge
in separate entries: every entry names exactly one decider, typed as `user:` or `agent:`, and
"which parts of this spec did a person choose?" is a filter, not an archaeology project.

## Extensions

| Term       | URI                          |
| ---------- | ---------------------------- |
| `decision` | `org.dxos.mdl.decision@1.0` |

## Actors

Every actor reference carries one of two prefixes. The prefix is the point of the dialect: the
linter rejects a `by:` without one.

| Form              | Who                                                   | Example                       |
| ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |
| `user:<handle>`   | A person, by GitHub username or email.                | `user:alice`, `user:a@b.org` |
| `agent:<name>`    | An agent, by harness or product name — never a model. | `agent:claude-code`           |

Name an agent by what ran it (`agent:claude-code`, `agent:composer-assistant`), not by the model
that served it: models change mid-session and the name must stay stable across the log.

## Rules

1. **Every change to a spec adds a decision.** A change to anything outside `## Changelog` comes
   with at least one new entry in the same change. The check
   `pnpm deus changelog check` enforces this in CI. Trivial edits are exempt:
   whitespace, and fixes of at most 3 words by 1–2 letters each, excluding words with digits and
   normative words. A bulk mechanical edit, or a change with no spec decision behind it (rule 9),
   may be waived with a `Spec-Changelog-Skip: <reason>` commit trailer.
2. **One decider per entry.** An entry is `by:` exactly one `user:` or `agent:`. Knowledge from
   two sources is two entries, even when they land in the same edit.
3. **A person's input is recorded faithfully, first.** When a person gives direction, the first
   entry is `by: user:<them>` and restates what they said in spec terms. The recording agent may
   resolve references ("it", "the canvas" → `Scene`), include the context needed to read the entry
   on its own, and fix wording. It must not add or remove information: no requirement, rationale or
   assumption the person did not give, and nothing they said left out. Prompts are messy, so the
   entry is a clean statement of their intent, not a transcript. The decider is who chose, not who
   typed: an agent writes this entry, the person made it.
4. **What the agent adds is a separate entry.** Gaps the agent filled — shapes, names, edge cases,
   a reading of an ambiguous instruction — go in later `by: agent:<name>` entries, one per
   independent choice, referring to the entry they build on (`Fills in [D-1]`).
5. **An agent entry names its principal.** `for:` is the person whose instruction the agent was
   acting under. It is required on `agent:` entries and forbidden on `user:` entries.
6. **Approval is a decision.** A person who reviews and accepts agent entries makes a new
   `user:` entry saying so ("Approve [D-2] and [D-3]."). Nothing in the approved entry changes.
7. **Append-only.** Never edit or delete an entry. To reverse or refine one, append a new entry
   with `supersedes: D-n`. The superseded entry stays — it is the trace.
8. **Identifiers are monotonic, newest last.** `D-1`, `D-2`, … in the order appended. Never reuse
   or renumber.
9. **Only spec decisions.** An entry records a choice about the specified system: its behavior,
   requirements, structure, interfaces, data, tests, or the reason for one of those. How the authors
   worked is not a decision: which tool or skill they used, how the spec is shared or reviewed, which
   PR a change goes in, that the document exists, has diagrams, or was reorganized. When a person's
   instruction is only about process, follow it and record nothing. A spec change with no decision
   behind it carries the `Spec-Changelog-Skip` trailer instead. `.agents/skills/mdl/scripts/check-decisions.ts` checks
   new entries with TypeSafe System One.

## Definitions

```mdl
ext decision
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.decision@1.0
  desc: |
    One decision about the spec, attributed to exactly one actor. The inline prose is the decision
    itself, stated as what is now true ("Bounds are derived, never stored."), not as the edit
    that was made.
  fields:
    date: Date                  # YYYY-MM-DD, the day the decision was made
    by: ActorRef                # user:<handle> | agent:<name> — the decider
    for?: UserRef               # agent entries only: the person the agent acted for
    affects?: RefList           # block ids the decision constrains, e.g. [Scene, F-2.1]
    supersedes?: Identifier     # an earlier decision this one replaces
    why?: Prose                 # the reason; on a user entry, only a reason the person gave
    rejected?: ProseList        # alternatives considered and why they lost
    source?: URI                # PR, review comment, issue, or session where it was made
  inline-prose: true
```

## Example

A person asks for a feature in one line; the agent records it, then fills it in; the person
approves part of it and overrides the rest.

```mdl
decision D-1: The `Scene` canvas grows to fit its content when a node moves past its edge.
  date: 2026-10-02
  by: user:alice
  affects: [Scene]
  source: https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/<n>
```

```mdl
decision D-2: Bounds are derived from content, never stored. Fills in [D-1].
  date: 2026-10-02
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:alice
  affects: [Scene]
  why: Stored bounds drift from the content whenever a node moves outside an edit path.
```

```mdl
decision D-3: Bounds grow to the major grid. Fills in [D-1].
  date: 2026-10-02
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:alice
  affects: [Scene, F-2.1]
  why: Growing to the minor grid re-lays the canvas on every drag step.
  rejected:
    - Fixed padding: clips large nodes at the edge.
```

```mdl
decision D-4: Approve [D-2].
  date: 2026-10-05
  by: user:alice
```

```mdl
decision D-5: Bounds grow to the minor grid while dragging and snap to the major grid on drop, because growing to the major grid feels laggy.
  date: 2026-10-05
  by: user:alice
  supersedes: D-3
```
